WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:21.289
Okay well I'm coming from the Pacific
time zone so I won't force you to be all

00:00:21.289 --> 00:00:28.279
awake and say hello to me. My name is
Sarah Kobilka and I too actually spent

00:00:28.279 --> 00:00:31.609
most of my life in the informal science
education realm working at science

00:00:31.609 --> 00:00:36.650
museums, running camps, really focusing on
that engage and inspire and the aha

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:41.690
moments and all that exciting stuff
until I transitioned recently into more

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:45.620
formal education role and actually just
finished a master's of science and

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:51.410
science education from the University of
Montana in Bozeman and it was an

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:55.309
opportunity for me to learn a little bit
more about the best practices of the

00:00:55.309 --> 00:00:58.700
formal education realm and I think we
can take some of those best practices

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:03.829
and maybe they have a role for us to
play. I know we like to think we informal

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:09.440
people got all the best stuff but I
would like to propose an idea called

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.649
backwards design and just by a show of
hands how many of you have heard of that

00:01:12.649 --> 00:01:17.600
concept? Excellent, okay, so we've heard of
it for those who haven't heard of it.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:22.160
Inspired by design by Grant Wiggins and
Jay McTighe is a wonderful book on that

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:27.110
topic it's a planning framework and on
the Vanderbilt website if you just do a

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:30.140
google search of backwards design you're
gonna find a video of Grant Wiggins

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:34.580
doing some teacher training and one of
the questions he says or one of the

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:41.240
statements he says is it's your job to
know where we want to end up and we just

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:45.830
said at the beginning how will I know
that this is successful and knowing what

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:52.160
the goal is is critical for designing
your program. You have to think from that

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:56.270
backwards design
backwards position because there's a

00:01:56.270 --> 00:02:02.179
disconnect from our short-term actions
and our long-term goals if we don't take

00:02:02.179 --> 00:02:08.720
that backwards position so one thing
that teachers often get challenged on is

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:13.250
that they teach to the test, that they
are focused on the content part, and

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:16.190
especially before the next generation
science standards came in and gave us

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:19.700
the science and engineering practices
and the cross-cutting concepts those

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:25.940
content strands really were everything
and so the concept that was come up with

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:28.850
the backwards design is that you have
three steps.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:33.890
You start with your end-to-end desire
your goals. Then you figure out how do

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:37.850
you assess to see if you've met those
goals and not just what you're going to

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:42.410
take a test or a quiz on in a formal
classroom but what skills do you want

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:46.250
people to have, what connections do you
want them to be able to make and then

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:51.890
finally they plan the learning
experience and instruction and in our

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:55.580
case in outreach, we might come up with
this really great idea of the activity

00:02:55.580 --> 00:03:00.110
that we're gonna do oh and then we have
to assess it and then maybe then I will

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:04.430
kind of think about what the goal was
and why we even did it and that

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:09.290
disconnect just doesn't work so the
questions that you want to be thinking

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:13.400
about with your goals and questions that I
don't have answers to but what do you

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:17.150
want your students to be able to do with
what they learn? What meanings or

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:22.100
transfers do you want to make and when
we go from that meaning angle it puts

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:25.460
power in the students because it implies
that they already know something and

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:29.090
they can take what you're teaching them
and experiences that you're giving them

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:33.920
and take them and apply them to
something that already matters to them

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:38.960
and when we talk about those goals that
we want to set we use the acronym smart.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:46.910
So S is specific M is measurable there's
those metrics again A achievable

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:52.250
you're not going to eradicate hunger but
you can make an achievable goal with

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:57.610
regards to that you have R relevant to
the program that you're doing and T

00:03:57.610 --> 00:04:02.030
time-bound you can't have a goal that's
just gonna continue going on forever.

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:06.380
You've got a funder, you've got a time
period, or even just a program you still

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:13.160
want to have times with that so specific
measurable achievable relevant to the

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:19.190
program and time-bound. Now going back to
when you set those goals I asked you the

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:25.160
question of who's at the table when you
start that planning process. Oftentimes I

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:31.370
do we do some work at the STEM Learning
Center with NSF funded programs and many

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:35.930
of you in this room have had people come
in and say okay my application is due in

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:40.160
one week can I can you write a letter of
support and you know help me out with my

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:42.729
broader impacts
because I really didn't think about it

00:04:42.729 --> 00:04:47.440
but I got my whole research ready to go.
So who is when can we get into that

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:51.490
conversation can we get in sooner who
else needs to be part of that

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:55.780
conversation when do you bring the
audience that you're trying to serve

00:04:55.780 --> 00:05:00.009
into the conversation especially if
you're talking about some students who

00:05:00.009 --> 00:05:04.150
are older if you're looking at working
with youth, high school kids have a lot

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:08.229
to say and they know what they want they
know what they're passionate about. They

00:05:08.229 --> 00:05:12.699
know what will mean something to them so
can you bring them to the table at a

00:05:12.699 --> 00:05:17.919
sooner at an earlier point we talked
about we had the concept of IRB just

00:05:17.919 --> 00:05:22.449
brought up well do you bring education
researchers do you work with your

00:05:22.449 --> 00:05:26.800
department with the College of Education
at your University or people who are

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:31.419
education researchers because there is a
difference between evaluating a program

00:05:31.419 --> 00:05:35.680
and doing education research on a
program and so these people can bring in

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:39.039
some expertise and maybe answer some of
those questions that we might have

00:05:39.039 --> 00:05:43.509
because that's not our area of expertise.
When do you bring in those informal

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:47.770
science educators: the people from the
museum, from the zoo, from the Boys and

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:52.659
Girls Club, not oh we've got this whole
idea now we want to bring it to your

00:05:52.659 --> 00:05:58.030
setting but hey come talk to us about
how we design something for your

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:02.349
settings so we don't make all these ideas
or come up with all the ideas and then

00:06:02.349 --> 00:06:08.650
we try to fit them into your setting. So
things to think about as you're doing it

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:13.840
when you have those groups together and
talking from the very beginning another

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:18.190
bonus that comes out of it is you get
that shared vocabulary and having that

00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:21.460
shared vocabulary then allows you to
have that communication on the topic

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:26.139
going forward and moving forward and
making it more of a sustainable not a

00:06:26.139 --> 00:06:29.949
one-off thing but truly building that
relationship and connecting you with

00:06:29.949 --> 00:06:34.659
those people in those audiences. Karen
asks the question what data is worth

00:06:34.659 --> 00:06:39.759
sharing I would ask this question what
data is worth collecting? Sometimes we

00:06:39.759 --> 00:06:43.810
put a lot of time and effort into
collecting all this stuff because well I

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:48.669
think it might be useful but really we
have limited resources and we have to

00:06:48.669 --> 00:06:53.229
acknowledge that so what is worth it for
us and for telling that story that was

00:06:53.229 --> 00:06:58.300
mentioned we need
the who the what the who the where the

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:03.270
what and the when those years sometimes
more metrics numerical things your

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:07.960
quantitative data but you need that
qualitative data as well to tell you the

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:12.430
how and the why. I can tell you that we
increase from thirty to sixty percent

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:16.810
but how did it happen? Why did it happen?
That's where that qualitative data comes

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:23.470
in in my personal feelings and so
connecting partners, audiences, education

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:28.870
researchers learning experiences to your
assessment which is tied to your goals.

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:34.510
All this concept of connectedness, if you
like talking about it well you are in

